choice ESPNOW_ROLE
    prompt "ESP-NOW firmware role"
    default ESPNOW_ROLE_RECEIVER

config ESPNOW_ROLE_SENDER
    bool "Sender (UART SML decoder and ESP-NOW value transport)"

config ESPNOW_ROLE_RECEIVER
    bool "Receiver (SML value transport and Wi-Fi dashboard)"

endchoice

config ESPNOW_CPU_MAX_MHZ
    int "Maximum CPU frequency (MHz) for the DFS ceiling"
    range 40 240
    default 80 if ESPNOW_ROLE_SENDER
    default 240
    help
        Upper clock for dynamic frequency scaling. The receiver is mains-powered
        and both the Wi-Fi dashboard and the minute-history backfill are
        CPU/SPIFFS bound, so its default stays at 240 MHz to keep the web UI
        responsive during a full re-sync.

        The sender defaults to 80 MHz: its Wi-Fi radio is held awake for a
        reliable ESP-NOW link (CONFIG_ESPNOW_SENDER_ESPNOW_POWER_SAVE is off by
        default), so the Wi-Fi driver holds ESP_PM_APB_FREQ_MAX and DFS cannot
        step below the ceiling anyway. Pinning the ceiling at 80 MHz is
        therefore the effective "DFS off, run at 80 MHz" setting and avoids the
        240 MHz heat/power the clock would otherwise never leave. The floor
        stays at the 40 MHz XTAL, so if the APB lock is ever released (e.g. the
        modem-sleep experiment) the clock can still drop to 40 MHz.

        Keep CONFIG_ESP_DEFAULT_CPU_FREQ_MHZ in sdkconfig.defaults at or above
        this value; power management lowers the boot clock to the ceiling.

config ESPNOW_SENDER_LIGHT_SLEEP
    bool "Sender: adaptive light sleep between SML telegram bursts"
    default y
    depends on ESPNOW_ROLE_SENDER
    help
        Let the sender park the CPU (light sleep) in the quiet gaps between
        SML telegram bursts. The window adapts to the shortest quiet interval
        the UART has shown and backs off when a sleep was too long. Light sleep
        stops the APB clock, so an actively receiving UART would lose bytes;
        the window is therefore only entered after the line has gone quiet.
        This also enables Wi-Fi modem sleep on the sender so the Wi-Fi driver
        releases its APB lock. Enabled by default: the sender ships as a single
        image. Verify on the bench that ESP-NOW broadcasts are still received
        while sleeping (see TODO.md); disable to fall back to DFS only.

config ESPNOW_SENDER_ESPNOW_WAKE_INTERVAL_MS
    int "ESP-NOW connectionless wake interval (ms)"
    range 10 1000
    default 100
    depends on ESPNOW_SENDER_ESPNOW_POWER_SAVE
    help
        ESP-NOW is a Wi-Fi "connectionless" module. On a sender whose station
        is not associated with any AP the Wi-Fi driver keeps ESP_PM_APB_FREQ_MAX
        asserted (RF/PHY/BB always on) unless a periodic wake window is set,
        which pins DFS and light sleep at the maximum clock forever (observed:
        "240 MHz, 0 steps, slept 0.0 s"). Setting an interval with a smaller
        window makes the driver release the radio (and the APB lock) between
        windows, so the CPU can scale down and light-sleep. The radio is awake
        for ESPNOW_SENDER_ESPNOW_WAKE_WINDOW_MS out of every interval; a request
        that arrives while the radio is off is simply retried by the receiver.

config ESPNOW_SENDER_ESPNOW_WAKE_WINDOW_MS
    int "ESP-NOW connectionless wake window (ms)"
    range 1 1000
    default 70
    depends on ESPNOW_SENDER_ESPNOW_POWER_SAVE
    help
        Must stay below the wake interval; a window >= interval keeps the radio
        on all the time and the APB lock stays pinned (no DFS, no light sleep).
        ~70% of the interval is a good trade-off between power saving and not
        dropping ESP-NOW requests from the receiver.

config ESPNOW_SENDER_ESPNOW_POWER_SAVE
    bool "Sender: ESP-NOW power save + DFS (experimental)"
    default n

    help
        Let the sender's Wi-Fi radio sleep between ESP-NOW wake windows so the
        driver releases ESP_PM_APB_FREQ_MAX and DFS can step down. OFF by
        default: on the ESP32 classic the wake window is accepted (err=0) but
        the radio still cycles, ESP-NOW requests are missed and the link
        watchdog disables the power saving again. Keeping the radio on (this
        option off) is what keeps the ESP-NOW link reliable, which matters more
        than the clock: the sender's data must not be lost.

        What this option does when on (verified on hardware):
          - esp_wifi_set_ps(WIFI_PS_MIN_MODEM) after esp_wifi_start(), and
          - esp_now_set_wake_window() + esp_wifi_connectionless_module_set_wake_interval(),
            applied from esp_now_init() and re-applied on every association.
        Both are needed: without the wake window the connectionless module holds
        the APB lock; without the explicit set_ps() call (after esp_wifi_start())
        the driver holds it too. With both, the radio does cycle - but on the
        ESP32 classic that breaks the ESP-NOW link, so expect the fallback.

        Requires CONFIG_ESP_WIFI_STA_DISCONNECTED_PM_ENABLE=y (set in
        sdkconfig.defaults) for the window to apply without an association.

config ESPNOW_SENDER_ASSOCIATE
    bool "Sender: also associate with the receiver SoftAP (optional)"
    default n

    help
        Associate the sender's station with the receiver's ESP32-IR-Meter
        SoftAP. This is optional: the DFS fix is the ESP-NOW wake window plus
        CONFIG_ESP_WIFI_STA_DISCONNECTED_PM_ENABLE (see
        ESPNOW_SENDER_ESPNOW_POWER_SAVE). Association on its own did NOT make
        the clock scale in a bench test, so it is off by default and kept only
        as an experiment. The station is otherwise unassociated (it just scans
        for the AP's channel). If enabled, the sender uses the receiver AP
        credentials below and re-associates after a drop; the ESP-NOW broadcast
        to the receiver AP's BSSID still works either way.
    depends on ESPNOW_ROLE_SENDER

config ESPNOW_SENDER_ASSOC_SSID
    string "Receiver SoftAP SSID for the sender to associate with"
    default "ESP32-IR-Meter"
    depends on ESPNOW_SENDER_ASSOCIATE

config ESPNOW_SENDER_ASSOC_PASSWORD
    string "Receiver SoftAP password for the sender to associate with"
    default "12345678"
    depends on ESPNOW_SENDER_ASSOCIATE

config ESPNOW_CHANNEL
    int "ESP-NOW channel"
    range 1 13
    default 1
    help
        Radio channel used for ESP-NOW. With the receiver's SoftAP enabled this
        is also the AP's channel and the sender scans for the AP to learn it
        automatically, so it rarely needs changing. With the AP disabled the
        sender can no longer discover the channel: both devices must be built
        with the same value here, and (station-connected receiver) it must match
        the channel of the home AP the receiver associates with, because a
        connected station cannot leave its AP's channel.

config ESPNOW_RECEIVER_DISABLE_AP
    bool "Receiver: disable the SoftAP (station-only dashboard)"
    default n
    depends on ESPNOW_ROLE_RECEIVER
    help
        Do not start the "ESP32-IR-Meter" SoftAP; keep the receiver in station
        mode only and serve the dashboard over the home WLAN (reachable by its
        DHCP address / mDNS). This removes the AP the sender used to find the
        channel, so the sender falls back to ESPNOW_CHANNEL and both sides must
        be built with the same ESPNOW_CHANNEL (see above). If no home WLAN is
        configured the dashboard is then unreachable; ESP-NOW still works.

config ESPNOW_LINK_FALLBACK
    bool "Both roles: fall back to the proven mode when the peer stops answering"
    default y
    help
        Each device watches the ESP-NOW link on its own: when no frame from the
        peer arrives for CONFIG_ESPNOW_LINK_FALLBACK_MS (and the uptime grace
        has passed), it returns to its proven mode. The sender turns its
        adaptive light sleep off for good and keeps the Wi-Fi radio awake; the
        receiver just records it in the diagnostics (a hung receiver is already
        handled by web_guard). The decision is sticky until the next boot, so it
        cannot flap between the new and the proven path. Independent per device:
        no handshake, so the two sides cannot deadlock.

config ESPNOW_LINK_FALLBACK_MS
    int "Peer silence before the link is declared down (ms)"
    depends on ESPNOW_LINK_FALLBACK
    range 5000 600000
    default 60000
    help
        How long no inbound frame may arrive before the fallback fires. Must be
        longer than the slowest expected poll interval on a healthy link (the
        dashboard polls the sender every few seconds), so a quiet but working
        link does not trip it.

config ESPNOW_LINK_FALLBACK_GRACE_MS
    int "Grace after boot before the link is judged (ms)"
    depends on ESPNOW_LINK_FALLBACK
    range 0 600000
    default 30000
    help
        Uptime that must pass before the fallback may fire, so a slow peer boot
        does not look like a dead link.

